uantur Phrygiī, Catulle, campī Nīcaeaeque ager ūber aestuōsae: ad clārās Asiae volēmus urbēs. Iam mēns praetrepidāns avet vagārī, iam laetī studiō pedēs vigēscunt. Ō dulcēs comitum valēte coetūs, lo…","Vēr novum, vēr iam canōrum, vēre nātus orbis est."]}],"f":[["vēr",211],["vēris",2],["vēr",7,3],["vēra",14,3],["vēris",3,3],["vērum",13,3],["vērī",6,3],["vēribus",12,3],["vēr",5,3],["vēra",9,3],["vēre",4,3],["vēribus",11,3],["vēr",8,3],["vēra",15,3]],"tpl":["la-ndecl"],"ipa":[{"i":"[ˈweːr]","t":1},{"i":"[ˈvɛr]","t":0}],"ety":{"text":"From Proto-Italic *wezor (stem *wezn-), from Proto-Indo-European *wósr̥ (“spring”). The original Italic form gave *veror, genitive *vēnis, with *-s- lost before -n- with lengthening of the preceding vowel, as is usual in Latin. The -n- of the genitive stem was then replaced by the -r- of the nominative, and the genitive stem was then extended back to the nominative. Cognate with Ancient Greek ἔᾰρ (éăr), Old Norse vár, Lithuanian vasara, Sanskrit वसर् (vasar, combining form of उ॒षस् (uṣás, “morning”)) and व॒स॒न्त (vasantá, “spring”), Persian بهار (bahâr, “spring”), Old Armenian գարուն (garun),…","tpl":[{"k":52,"lang":56,"form":"*wezor"},{"k":52,"lang":48,"form":"*wósr̥","gloss":"spring"},{"k":42,"lang":45,"form":"ἔᾰρ"},{"k":42,"lang":124,"form":"vár"},{"k":42,"lang":"lt","form":"vasara"},{"k":42,"lang":"sa","form":"वसर्"},{"k":42,"lang":"fa","form":"بهار","gloss":"spring"},{"k":42,"lang":111,"form":"գարուն"},{"k":42,"lang":"cu","form":"весна"}]},"rel":{"d":["prīmum vēr"],"r":["vē̆rnālis","vē̆rnō","vē̆rnus"]}}]}}
